# Colour, fill and alpha: aes_colour_fill_alpha

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

economics = Giggly.Datasets.economics()
mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`aes_colour_fill_alpha`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/aes_colour_fill_alpha.html), in
the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An
example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the
page is also a list of what remains.

`colour` and `fill` give a geom its colours, and `alpha` its opacity. `colour` draws lines and
outlines: points, lines, and the borders of rectangles and polygons. `fill` colours the inside of an
area: rectangles, polygons, and the point shapes 21 to 25. A colour is one of R's named colours,
such as `"red"`, an RGB specification, `"#RRGGBB"`, which may add an alpha as `"#RRGGBBAA"`, or
`NA`, for no colour at all; `alpha` runs from 0, transparent, to 1, opaque. Giggly has all three,
mapped to a column or set to a value, though no point that takes a fill yet, a set colour written as
CSS writes one: a name, a hex colour, or `:none` for `NA`. Where R and CSS give a name different
colours, as `"green"`, it is CSS's, and a name CSS lacks, as R's `"grey50"`, is written as its hex.
A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG
document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines
`render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. It binds
`Giggly.Datasets.economics/0`, ggplot2's `economics`, and `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's
`mtcars`, to `economics` and `mtcars`, the names ggplot2 and R give them.

## The default bars

```r
# Bar chart example
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(factor(cyl)))
# Default plotting
p + geom_bar()
```

`factor(cyl)` makes the number of cylinders discrete, so the axis has a place for each of 4, 6
and 8. Giggly writes it `{:factor, [:cyl]}`, and titles the axis `factor(cyl)`, as ggplot2 does.

```elixir
p = Plot.new(mtcars, %{x: {:factor, [:cyl]}})

p
|> Plot.geom_bar()
|> render.()
```

## Fill and outline colour

```r
# To change the interior colouring use fill aesthetic
p + geom_bar(fill = "red")
# Compare with the colour aesthetic which changes just the bar outline
p + geom_bar(colour = "red")
# Combining both, you can see the changes more clearly
p + geom_bar(fill = "white", colour = "red")
```

`fill:` and `colour:` set every bar's inside and border; `"red"` and `"white"` are the same
colours in CSS as in R.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_bar(fill: "red")
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_bar(colour: "red")
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_bar(fill: "white", colour: "red")
|> render.()
```

## RGB colours, and none

```r
# Both colour and fill can take an rgb specification.
p + geom_bar(fill = "#00abff")
# Use NA for a completely transparent colour.
p + geom_bar(fill = NA, colour = "#00abff")
```

A hex colour is written as R writes it, and `:none` is R's `NA`, no colour at all.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_bar(fill: "#00abff")
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_bar(fill: :none, colour: "#00abff")
|> render.()
```

## Mapping fill to a discrete column

```r
# Colouring scales differ depending on whether a discrete or
# continuous variable is being mapped. For example, when mapping
# fill to a factor variable, a discrete colour scale is used.
ggplot(mtcars, aes(factor(cyl), fill = factor(vs))) + geom_bar()
```

`factor(vs)` reads the engine shape, 0 or 1, as two levels, so the fill takes a discrete scale and
each bar a stack of two.

```elixir
mtcars
|> Plot.new(%{x: {:factor, [:cyl]}, fill: {:factor, [:vs]}})
|> Plot.geom_bar()
|> render.()
```

## Mapping fill to a continuous column

```r
# When mapping fill to continuous variable a continuous colour
# scale is used.
ggplot(faithfuld, aes(waiting, eruptions)) +
  geom_raster(aes(fill = density))
```

Not in Giggly yet: the `faithfuld` dataset and `geom_raster()`.

## Geoms with colour only

```r
# Some geoms only use the colour aesthetic but not the fill
# aesthetic (e.g. geom_point() or geom_line()).
p <- ggplot(economics, aes(x = date, y = unemploy))
p + geom_line()
p + geom_line(colour = "green")
p + geom_point()
p + geom_point(colour = "red")
```

A line and points take `colour:`, and have no fill to set. A colour is named as CSS names it, and
`"green"` is CSS's, `#008000`, darker than R's, `#00FF00`, so this line is darker than ggplot2's;
`"red"` is `#FF0000` in both.

```elixir
p = Plot.new(economics, %{x: :date, y: :unemploy})

p
|> Plot.geom_line()
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_line(colour: "green")
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_point(colour: "red")
|> render.()
```

## Transparency for overplotting

```r
# For large datasets with overplotting the alpha
# aesthetic will make the points more transparent.
set.seed(1)
df <- data.frame(x = rnorm(5000), y = rnorm(5000))
p  <- ggplot(df, aes(x,y))
p + geom_point()
p + geom_point(alpha = 0.5)
p + geom_point(alpha = 1/10)
```

R's `rnorm()` draws the points at random from a normal distribution, the same ones every time
after `set.seed(1)`.

Giggly draws all three now, but this notebook leaves them undrawn: it would have to write out the
10,000 numbers `rnorm()` draws here.

## Shading with alpha

```r
# Alpha can also be used to add shading.
p <- ggplot(economics, aes(x = date, y = unemploy)) + geom_line()
p
yrng <- range(economics$unemploy)
p <- p +
  geom_rect(
    aes(NULL, NULL, xmin = start, xmax = end, fill = party),
    ymin = yrng[1], ymax = yrng[2], data = presidential
  )
p
p + scale_fill_manual(values = alpha(c("blue", "red"), .3))
```

The rectangles span each president's term, from the `presidential` table, coloured by party and
as tall as the range of unemployment.

Not in Giggly yet: the `presidential` dataset, removing the plot's `x` and `y` from a layer's
mapping, R's `aes(NULL, NULL)`, and `scale_fill_manual()` with colours made transparent by
`alpha()`.